##======== /etc/anon/anonrc — the sandbox's HIDDEN-SERVICE ingress ==========##
#
# The daemon that gives the anytoon issuer path an address. It generates the
# address, publishes a descriptor for it on the REAL Anyone network, and
# forwards what arrives to the anytoon-connector's client edge. It opens no
# SOCKS port: this daemon is only ever dialed. The buyer's way ONTO the network
# is a second daemon — see conf/anonrc-client.
#
# Vendored from the anytoon sibling checkout's config/anonrc, which is the
# reference implementation. Only the two HiddenServicePort targets differ, and
# the block above them says why.
#
# The connector NEVER READS THIS FILE and never speaks this daemon's control
# protocol (connector ADR 0070 decision 7). It is a sidecar, in the relationship
# this sandbox already has with a chain node: infrastructure a node talks to,
# never something it contains. What crosses between them is one TCP connection
# and one address, copied by scripts/hs-address.sh.
#
# Mounted READ-ONLY at /etc/anon/anonrc.

# WITHOUT THIS THE DAEMON DOES NOT START. `anon` refuses to run until the terms
# are accepted, and in a non-interactive container it fails fast —
# "User has not agreed to the terms and conditions. Exiting." — rather than
# prompting. It is the most common reason a first bring-up leaves a container
# that exited at once beside a connector that looks healthy and is unreachable.
AgreeToTerms 1

# Drop from root to the image's own unprivileged user, whose home the
# DataDirectory is. The entrypoint has already chowned it.
User anond
DataDirectory /var/lib/anon

# EXPLICIT, and it cannot be dropped: the image's entrypoint APPENDS a Nickname
# when it finds none, and this file is mounted read-only — so a missing one is a
# write failure at boot rather than a default.
Nickname toonsandboxhs

# A SERVICE, not a relay and not a client. This node has no peers and no
# pay-channels of its own, so it opens no circuits for anyone else and needs no
# SOCKS port. Nothing routes through it either.
ClientOnly 1
SocksPort 0
ORPort 0
DirPort 0
ControlSocket 0

# --- The address, and where it lives ----------------------------------------
#
# `anon` writes the generated address to HiddenServiceDir/hostname and keeps the
# private key beside it. On v0.4.10.2 that address ends in `.anyone`, NOT
# `.onion` — the TLD was renamed, the rename is total, and @toon-protocol/client
# accepts `.anyone` alone. See anon/Dockerfile.
#
# THIS DIRECTORY IS INSIDE THE NAMED VOLUME compose mounts at /var/lib/anon.
# In a DEPLOYMENT that persistence is load-bearing and its loss is expensive:
# the address changes and every buyer's configuration goes stale silently. In
# THIS SANDBOX it is deliberately disposable — `make down` keeps it (so a
# restart keeps the address), `make clean` wipes it with every other volume, and
# a new address on the next cold start is expected rather than a fault. That is
# the one way a rehearsal is easier than the thing it rehearses.
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/anon/hidden_service

# --- The two virtual ports, and why they are 127.0.0.1 ----------------------
#
# `anon` resolves these targets when it PARSES its config, not when a stream
# arrives — so a container name here aborts the daemon before it ever runs if
# that container does not exist yet:
#
#     [warn] Unparseable address in hidden service port configuration.
#     free(): invalid size
#
# ...and it cannot exist yet, because the anytoon-connector's own config has to
# name an address only THIS daemon can generate. The daemon must start first.
# anytoon breaks that circle with a pinned subnet and a fixed container IP; this
# sandbox breaks it with a loopback the daemon shares — `anon` runs inside the
# `hs-ingress` container's network namespace, and that container forwards these
# two loopback ports on to `anytoon-connector` and `anvil` by NAME, resolved per
# connection. Nothing needs a fixed IP, no existing service is touched, and
# recreating the connector (which `make up-hs` does, to give it the rendered
# config) cannot leave this daemon pointed at a dead address.
#
# PORT 80 — THE ISSUER PATH. `client_edge_addr = "0.0.0.0:3000"` is the single
# socket the anytoon-connector binds, and `POST /ilp` on it is the client edge a
# buyer pays through. An address is a host, so it is addressed the way any host
# is: port 80, and the path decides the rest.
HiddenServicePort 80 127.0.0.1:3000

# PORT 8545 — THE CHAIN RPC, on the SAME address and the same circuit.
#
# This is not a second ingress; it is what makes the first one honest. The TOON
# client sends EVM JSON-RPC through `socksProxy` BY DEFAULT (proxyRpc), because
# reaching a connector inside the overlay while reading chain state on clearnet
# would broadcast the payer's settlement address, from the payer's own IP, timed
# either side of every paid request (toon-client ADR 0002). A buyer settling
# against this sandbox's `anvil` therefore needs anvil reachable over the
# overlay too — and the alternative, `proxyRpc: false`, is exactly the property
# the rehearsal exists to exercise. One address, one descriptor, one key, two
# virtual ports: `http://<addr>.anyone` is the connector and
# `http://<addr>.anyone:8545` is the chain it settles on.
HiddenServicePort 8545 127.0.0.1:8545

# PORTS 3200 AND 7100 — THE HUB AND THE RELAY, for the HIDDEN PROVIDER
# (TOON_Network #43). They are on THIS daemon's address rather than the hidden
# provider's own, and that is the whole point of putting them here.
#
# A Hidden Provider dials every relay and its payer's connector through its own
# `anon` SOCKS port (spec §10 story 8), and `anon` builds no circuit to a
# private address — which is what `relay:7100` and `relay-connector:3000` are on
# this compose network. In a deployment the hub and the relays are on clearnet
# and an exit reaches them. This sandbox has no clearnet, so they are reached
# over the overlay instead; and they are published HERE because a daemon
# connecting to ITS OWN hidden service is the one overlay path that is
# unreliable in practice — the path restrictions that keep a rendezvous
# anonymous exclude the relays it is already using, and it can sit in
# "waiting for circuit" until it gives up. Two daemons, an ordinary
# client-to-service circuit, and the property under test is unchanged: the
# provider's packets leave over a circuit and arrive naming nothing about it.
#
# Both targets are the loopback this daemon shares with `hs-ingress`, like the
# two above, so `socat` resolves the service names per connection.
HiddenServicePort 3200 127.0.0.1:3200
HiddenServicePort 7100 127.0.0.1:7100

# Twice, deliberately: stdout is where `docker compose logs` reads, and the file
# is what the healthcheck greps for `Bootstrapped 100%`. A container that is Up
# is not a container that has a circuit.
Log notice stdout
Log notice file /var/lib/anon/notice.log
